---
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import { editorialPolicy } from "../lib/category-editorial";
import { pageGraphSchema } from "../lib/seo";
import { siteConfig } from "../lib/site";

const path = "/policy";
const title = `Inclusion policy | ${siteConfig.name}`;
const pageName = "Agent-first inclusion policy";
const description =
  "How agentfirst.directory checks product fit before evidence review and classification.";

const structuredData = [
  pageGraphSchema({
    path,
    name: pageName,
    description,
    type: "WebPage",
    datePublished: editorialPolicy.effectiveDate,
    dateModified: editorialPolicy.effectiveDate,
    lastReviewed: editorialPolicy.effectiveDate,
    breadcrumbs: [
      { name: siteConfig.shortName, path: "/" },
      { name: "Policy", path },
    ],
  }),
];
---

<BaseLayout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  canonicalPath={path}
  structuredData={structuredData}
>
  <div class="shell-inner policy-shell">
    <section class="page-intro page-intro-centered">
      <p class="detail-overline">
        Editorial policy {editorialPolicy.version} · Effective {editorialPolicy.effectiveDateLabel}
      </p>
      <h1 class="page-title">What belongs in an agent-first directory</h1>
      <p class="page-copy">
        Only tools built for agents belong in this directory. Product fit is decided before evidence review.
        Passing fit is necessary, not acceptance: each listing must then have public evidence for its claimed role
        and fit exactly one classification.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="detail-panel policy-panel">
      <p class="detail-overline">Working definition</p>
      <h2>Built for agents, not merely callable by agents</h2>
      <p>
        Judge the intended user, core job, and operating model. Agent-oriented infrastructure, protocols, and
        agent-builder tools can qualify when designed for agent operation; exclusive agent use or agent-only origins
        are not required. An AI agent serving humans is not, by itself, a tool built for agents. A human-first app
        with REST, MCP, or SDK access does not qualify, regardless of integration depth or tool count.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="policy-classification-grid" id="classification-model" aria-labelledby="classifications-heading">
      <h2 id="classifications-heading" class="visually-hidden">The three classifications</h2>

      <article class="detail-panel policy-classification-card">
        <p class="detail-overline">Agent-native</p>
        <h3>Agents are core participants</h3>
        <p>
          The product's core abstraction, actor, runtime, or participant is an agent. Removing agents would
          fundamentally change what the product is.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Example:</strong> an orchestrator that assigns work across agent runtimes, or a service that provisions
          persistent communication identities for agents.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Counterexample:</strong> a conventional task manager or inbox with a generic API that an agent can call.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="detail-panel policy-classification-card">
        <p class="detail-overline">Agent-enabling</p>
        <h3>Infrastructure materially empowers agents</h3>
        <p>
          Infrastructure or builder tooling is designed for agent operation and gives agents meaningful capabilities,
          even when people and conventional applications can also use it. This classification cannot bypass product
          fit or rescue a human-first app through its integrations.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Example:</strong> an isolated execution environment designed for agent-controlled code runs, or a
          memory layer that preserves and retrieves agent state across sessions.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Counterexample:</strong> a general virtual machine, database, or storage API with no substantive
          agent-oriented capability.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="detail-panel policy-classification-card">
        <p class="detail-overline">Agent internet protocol</p>
        <h3>Protocols let agents participate online</h3>
        <p>
          An interoperable protocol or standard enables agents to communicate, transact, identify, coordinate,
          or interact across the internet.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Example:</strong> an open specification for agent-user events or machine-readable payment negotiation
          with independently implementable messages and behaviour.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Counterexample:</strong> a vendor-specific integration endpoint or proprietary payload without an
          interoperable specification.
        </p>
      </article>
    </section>

    <section class="detail-panel policy-panel" id="inclusion-test">
      <p class="detail-overline">Product-fit-first inclusion test</p>
      <h2>Product fit before evidence</h2>
      <p>
        Before reviewing any evidence, use only the product description and operating model, plus canonical homepage
        positioning if necessary. Do not inspect evidence arrays, documentation, MCP details, implementation, pricing,
        licence, or validation results at this stage. Record the intended user, core job, and why it is or is not
        built for agents, with one decision:
      </p>
      <ul class="policy-test-list">
        <li><strong>pass-fit:</strong> proceed to full first-party evidence, schema, and contradiction review.</li>
        <li><strong>fail-fit:</strong> stop with nonacceptance; do not review evidence, enrich metadata, or commit listing changes.</li>
        <li><strong>unclear-fit:</strong> hold for product-positioning clarification; do not review evidence or proceed with metadata or listing commits.</li>
      </ul>
      <h3>Only after pass-fit</h3>
      <ol class="policy-test-list">
        <li><strong>Identify the agent role.</strong> State what agents do and why that role is material.</li>
        <li><strong>Use first-party evidence.</strong> Verify the claim in product docs, a maintained repository, a specification, or the official website.</li>
        <li><strong>Test materiality.</strong> Generic API access or ordinary automation is not enough; the capability must substantially improve an agent-first workflow.</li>
        <li><strong>Choose one classification.</strong> Select the most specific class supported by the evidence, not the most flattering label.</li>
      </ol>
      <p>
        Category and classification answer different questions. A category describes the capability area; a
        classification explains why the listing belongs in an agent-first directory.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="detail-panel policy-panel policy-exclusions">
      <p class="detail-overline">What does not qualify</p>
      <h2>Compatibility alone is insufficient</h2>
      <ul class="policy-test-list">
        <li>A generic developer tool that an agent could happen to call.</li>
        <li>A human-first app with a secondary REST API, MCP server, or SDK, however extensive.</li>
        <li>A product whose only agent role is internal AI completing a human customer's task.</li>
        <li>Unsupported marketing claims without first-party product evidence.</li>
        <li>A conventional workflow with “AI” or “agent” added only as positioning.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        A coding-agent orchestrator qualifies only when the system owns a material lifecycle across agent runs. Evidence
        must show task assignment, isolation, retained or shared state or validation evidence, and a completion, review,
        or landing gate separate from execution. Merely launching a single agent, supplying prompts, wrapping an API or
        MCP server, adding agent compatibility, or providing ordinary developer automation is insufficient.
      </p>
      <p>
        Classification describes why an accepted listing belongs. It is not a quality score, endorsement, or
        substitute for reviewing security, reliability, pricing, and fitness for a particular use.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="detail-panel policy-panel">
      <p class="detail-overline">Accountability</p>
      <h2>How this policy is applied</h2>
      <p>
        Read the <a class="detail-link" href="/editorial-standards">editorial standards</a> for source hierarchy,
        conflicts, sponsorship, ordering, and review cadence. If a published claim is wrong or a product has changed,
        use the <a class="detail-link" href="/corrections">corrections and appeals process</a>. Policy changes are
        versioned from the effective date shown above. This product-fit gate takes precedence over category or
        contribution guidance suggesting internal autonomous agents alone qualify. Historical reassessments and
        public corrections require separate owner approval; this policy update authorizes no existing-listing changes.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="policy-cta">
      <p>Have an evidence-backed tool that passes the test?</p>
      <a class="button button-primary" href="/submit">Read the submission guide</a>
    </section>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
